Subject: JSON.parse without a reviver reads values straight from the bytes, the general parser redoing any input it does not finish

What this does
  - `json_obj_begin` / `json_obj_add`: the object building of `json_parse_value` (the depth's shape cache, adopting the expected shape, rebuilding the chain on a divergence) as functions, used by both parsers; the chain is extended only at its end
  - `json_fast_value` / `json_fast_parse`: a recursive parser over the bytes for the no-reviver case, with no token stream (no position bookkeeping, no token freed per punctuation mark): whitespace, the three literals, strings, keys (with the expected-key hint) and numbers through the general parser's own readers, arrays staged and built in one shot, objects through `json_obj_add`. On anything it does not expect, a syntax error included, it frees what it built and gives up; an exception one of the readers threw is cleared (an uncatchable one is returned)
  - `js_json_parse` tries it first on both the 8-bit and the UTF-8 input, and parses with the general parser when it gave up

Why we need it
  json_next_token and json_parse_value (token state, line and column bookkeeping, token frees, the dispatch per punctuation mark) were about 31% of parse cycles on the app-traffic benchmark. json_app parse only: -16.6% cycles, -19.4% instructions (after 0583).

Soundness
  The direct parser accepts a subset of what the general parser accepts (JSON whitespace only, the same readers for strings, keys and numbers, the same literal bytes, the same structure) and builds the value with the same object code, so an input it finishes gives the same value; every other input, including every invalid one, is parsed from the start by the general parser, which throws exactly as before. Differentials, old engine vs new: the 160,000 JSON inputs and the 400,015 numbers of 0581-0583, and 47 edge inputs (100,000-deep nesting, 18,000-byte nested objects, every JSON and non-JSON whitespace, NUL after the value, 100 KB strings, 50,000-element arrays, escaped keys equal to plain ones, malformed numbers and literals, truncated inputs, astral and 16-bit text) give identical results and error messages.

